<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="EN">
	<head>
		{{ template "common_meta" . }}
	</head>

	<body>
		{{ template "common_heading_start" }}
		{{ template "common_heading_end" }}

		<h2>Help</h2>
		<p>
			SwitchGopher polls your network switches over SNMP and shows what is
			plugged into each port. Everything here is read-only: the pages report
			what the switches said the last time they were polled.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><a href="#finding">Finding things</a></li>
			<li><a href="#pages">The pages</a></li>
			<li><a href="#switch">Inside a switch</a></li>
			<li><a href="#data">Where the data comes from</a></li>
			<li><a href="#faq">Questions and gotchas</a></li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="finding">Finding things</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><b>Know the switch?</b> Open <a href="/campus/switches/">Devices</a> and pick it from the list. Narrow the list with the Category, Building, Room, Model and Number drop-downs; each one applies as soon as you choose it.</li>
			<li><b>Know a MAC address or hostname?</b> Type part of it into the search box in the menu bar and press GO. Search matches any part of a MAC address or hostname, and lists where it was seen: the date, switch, port, CDP neighbor, IP and host.</li>
			<li><b>Looking for a VLAN?</b> Open <a href="/campus/vlans/">Vlans</a> and click the VLAN number.</li>
			<li><b>Want a free port?</b> Open <a href="/campus/unused/">Unused</a>.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="pages">The pages</h2>
		<p><b><a href="/campus/home/">Home</a></b> is the landing page.</p>
		<p>
			<b><a href="/campus/switches/">Devices</a></b> lists every switch, filtered
			by the drop-downs at the top. Click a switch to see its ports.
		</p>
		<p>
			<b><a href="/campus/vlans/">Vlans</a></b> lists each VLAN with its name,
			subnet, how many devices and ports use it, and its utilization. Sort by
			VLAN number or name, and tick "Show unused vlans" to include VLANs that no
			port currently uses. Click a VLAN to see which ports belong to it.
		</p>
		<p>
			<b><a href="/campus/stats/">Stats</a></b> gives network-wide totals: switch
			and module counts by model, ports by state, trunk, EtherChannel and IP
			phone port counts, and a per-switch breakdown of active, inactive and
			disabled ports.
		</p>
		<p>
			<b><a href="/campus/unused/">Unused</a></b> lists ports that have been
			inactive for longer than the configured threshold (60 days by default),
			with how many days each has been idle. Filter by building, room or
			switch.
		</p>
		<p><b>Logout</b> (top right) ends your session.</p>

		<h2 id="switch">Inside a switch</h2>
		<p>Opening a switch gives you four tabs:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><b>Ports</b> &ndash; one row per port, described below.</li>
			<li><b>Info</b> &ndash; model, firmware, location, contact, address, uptime and, for chassis switches, the installed modules.</li>
			<li><b>Configs</b> &ndash; saved copies of the switch's configuration, if any have been provided.</li>
			<li><b>Diffs</b> &ndash; what changed between saved configurations.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Reading the Ports table</h2>
		<p>The left half describes the port; the right half describes what is plugged into it.</p>
		<ul>
			<li><b>Port</b> &ndash; the interface number. Click it for the port's detail page, including traffic and error counters.</li>
			<li><b>Vlan / Aux</b> &ndash; the data VLAN, and the voice (auxiliary) VLAN if one is set.</li>
			<li><b>Mode</b> &ndash; Access (one VLAN, normally an end device) or Trunk (carries many VLANs, normally another switch).</li>
			<li><b>State</b> &ndash; whether the link is up right now. <b>Admin State</b> is whether someone has switched the port on or off in the configuration; a disabled port is always down.</li>
			<li><b>Since</b> &ndash; when the port last changed state.</li>
			<li><b>Speed / Duplex</b> &ndash; the negotiated link speed and duplex.</li>
			<li><b>PoE</b> &ndash; whether the port is delivering Power over Ethernet, and its power class.</li>
			<li><b>Aggregate</b> &ndash; the EtherChannel (link bundle) the port belongs to. A second table below the ports lists each bundle's members.</li>
			<li><b>Description</b> &ndash; the label configured on the port.</li>
			<li><b>CDP</b> &ndash; the neighbor device Cisco Discovery Protocol reported on this port, usually another switch or an access point.</li>
			<li><b>NIC / MAC / IP / Host</b> &ndash; the device seen on the port: its network-card vendor (from the MAC address), MAC, IP address and hostname.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="data">Where the data comes from</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><b>Switches</b> are polled over SNMP on a schedule (every 5 minutes by default), so a page can be a few minutes behind reality.</li>
			<li><b>IP addresses</b> come from a router's ARP table and <b>hostnames</b> from reverse DNS, so either can be blank for a device the router has not seen recently.</li>
			<li><b>Sightings</b> of devices on ports are kept for 90 days by default, then removed.</li>
			<li><b>Buildings, rooms, categories and VLAN subnets</b> come from the site's configuration files, not from SNMP, since switches cannot report them.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="faq">Questions and gotchas</h2>
		<p><b>A port shows no MAC address, but I know something is plugged in.</b>
			The switch only learns a MAC address when the device sends traffic.
			A quiet device, or one the switch has aged out, will show up blank.</p>
		<p><b>A port shows several MAC addresses.</b>
			The port probably leads to another switch, a hub, a VoIP phone with a PC
			behind it, or a virtualization host. Trunk ports usually show many.</p>
		<p><b>A switch is missing from Devices.</b>
			Devices are added by an administrator through the configuration files.
			Ask them to add it.</p>
		<p><b>The Configs and Diffs tabs say "not available".</b>
			SwitchGopher does not collect configurations itself yet. They appear once
			someone drops saved copies into the configuration folder.</p>
		<p><b>A port's numbers look wrong or a column is empty.</b>
			Not every switch reports every value (PoE, aggregation and counters vary
			by model and firmware). A blank means the switch did not report it.</p>
		<p><b>I was sent back to the login page.</b>
			Sessions last 12 hours and end whenever the server restarts. Log in again.</p>

		{{ template "common_footer" . }}
	</body>
</html>
